const faq = [
  { q: 'What does Muninn mean?', a: 'Muninn (Old Norse: <em>Muninn</em>) derives from the Old Norse noun <em>munr</em> (also <em>muna</em>, "to remember"), related to memory, desire, and the capacity to retain experience. The name is standardly translated as "Memory." The root is Proto-Germanic <em>*munaz</em>, related to English "mind," Latin <em>mens</em>, and Sanskrit <em>manas</em> — all from Proto-Indo-European <em>*men-</em> ("to think, remember").' },
  { q: 'Why does Odin worry more for Muninn?', a: 'Grímnismál stanza 20 states Odin fears for Huginn but worries more for Muninn. Scholars have interpreted this as reflecting the Norse view that memory is more fundamental than thought: without memory, nothing learned can be retained; without thought, one can still draw on what is remembered. Memory sustains identity and accumulated wisdom over time, making its loss more catastrophic than the loss of active thinking. This interpretation is widely accepted but not beyond debate — the stanza is terse and admits multiple readings.' },
  { q: 'Is Muninn the same as Odin\'s personal memory?', a: 'Some scholars read Huginn and Muninn as externalisations of Odin\'s own cognitive capacities — his thought and memory taking bird form. On this reading, Muninn represents Odin\'s accumulated knowledge, his memory of everything that has occurred across the nine worlds. When Odin fears for Muninn, he fears losing his accumulated wisdom and knowledge of history. This psychoanalytic or allegorical reading is influential but should be read alongside the narrative presentation: in the Eddas, the ravens are also literally birds who fly and return.' },
];
